"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][21]={"university_physics_en:M21-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M21-L1 – Seismic Waves, Travel Times and Earth Structure<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Bachelor: Geophysics and Climate<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4: wave physics; M17: model\nfitting.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish P and S waves,\ncalculate travel times and explain basic limits of Earth-interior\ntomography.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>An earthquake generates elastic disturbances that reach measuring\nstations as temporally separated signals. A compressional P wave usually\narrives first, while a shear S wave tends to arrive later. Their travel\ntimes carry information about the material along their paths. A\nseismogram is a measurement of ground motion, not a direct picture of\nsubsurface geology. Inferring structure from arrival times requires a\nmodel of wave speeds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For a homogeneous, isotropic and linearly elastic material of density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbulk modulus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand shear modulus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe bulk-wave speeds are approximately\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_P=\\sqrt{\\frac{K+4\\mu/3}{\\rho}},\\qquad v_S=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu}{\\rho}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nShear waves require\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nan ideal fluid with vanishing shear rigidity therefore does not support\nbulk S waves. For a straight ray of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe simplest forward model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=d/v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the same path length is assumed, the P-to-S arrival delay is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=t_S-t_P=d(1/v_S-1/v_P)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a stratified Earth, gradients refract rays and boundaries produce\nreflections or conversions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>In a synthetic example, a seismic ray follows an idealised path of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=120\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_P=6\\,\\mathrm{km/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_S=3.5\\,\\mathrm{km/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe P arrival occurs after\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">20.0\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the S arrival after about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>34.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">34.3\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nleaving a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">14.3\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndelay. The illustration shows two artificially generated arrival pulses\nrather than measured earthquake records.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_260_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two synthetic seismic waveforms show an earlier P arrival and a later S arrival.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two synthetic seismic waveforms show an\nearlier P arrival and a later S arrival.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Idealised seismogram with synthetic arrival times; not\nobservational data.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=50\\,\\mathrm{GPa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=30\\,\\mathrm{GPa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=3000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nray, find the S–P delay if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_P=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_S=3\\,\\mathrm{km/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does one S–P delay not uniquely locate an earthquake source in\nthree dimensions?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>9<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3000<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>5.48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_P=\\sqrt{(50+40)\\cdot10^9/3000}\\approx5.48\\,\\mathrm{km/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=90/3-90/6=15\\,\\mathrm{s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Without further stations, a velocity model and an origin time, it\nconstrains distance but not the full source position.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The calculation assumes one path length through uniform, isotropic\nrock. Attenuation, scattering, reflections, source depth and velocity\nchanges alter actual signals. A teaching diagram is not a geophysical\ntomographic reconstruction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Seismic arrival times constrain Earth structure indirectly, through a\ntravel-time and material model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Gravity, Magnetic Fields and\nGeophysical Anomalies<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-201-essentials-of-geophysics-fall-2004/resources/ch4/\">MIT OCW – Essentials of Geophysics – ch4.pdf (Ressourcenseite)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 121; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L1 – Seismic Waves, Travel Times and Earth Structure · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-201-essentials-of-geophysics-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Essentials of Geophysics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: ch. 2, lines 136–146; chapter outline; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L1 – Seismic Waves, Travel Times and Earth Structure · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M21-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M21-L2 – Gravity, Magnetic Fields and Geophysical Anomalies<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Bachelor: Geophysics and Climate<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M3: gravitation; M6: magnetic fields;\nM17: inverse models. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain how physical property\ncontrasts generate measured field anomalies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Gravity and magnetic field surveys reveal subsurface contrasts even\nwhen the rocks themselves cannot be seen. A measured departure from a\ncomputed reference field is called an anomaly. Excess mass may generate\na positive gravity anomaly; magnetic signatures respond instead to\nmagnetisation and field orientation. Neither instrument provides a\nunique geological map without additional modelling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>Newtonian point-mass potential per unit test mass is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Phi=-Gm/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand gravitational acceleration is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒈<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol g=-\\nabla\\Phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a small hidden excess mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat depth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbelow a level survey line, the downward vertical acceleration anomaly in\nan idealised point-source model is\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta g_z(x)=\\frac{G\\Delta m\\,h}{(x^2+h^2)^{3/2}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nAt sufficiently large distances, the magnetic field of a local\nmagnetised body may be approximated by a dipole, whose response also\ndepends on orientation. Measurements combine regional background,\ntarget, geometry and instrumental response; practical surveys require\nreference and elevation corrections.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>11<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m=10^{11}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=1000\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDirectly over the source,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.67<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.667<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta g_z(0)=G\\Delta m/h^2\\approx6.67\\cdot10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\approx0.667\\,\\mathrm{mGal}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{mGal}=10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure compares this synthetic, symmetric gravity response with a\nqualitative bipolar magnetic response. The magnetic trace is not derived\nfrom the same mass-only model.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_076_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A synthetic positive gravity anomaly and a qualitative magnetic dipole response along a survey line.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A synthetic positive gravity anomaly and\na qualitative magnetic dipole response along a survey line.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Contrasting model field responses; not a geophysical field\nsurvey.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta g_z(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstays fixed?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta g_z(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin mGal for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m=5\\cdot10^{10}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=1000\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can different subsurface models yield similar observed\nanomalies?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta g_z(0)\\propto h^{-2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit becomes one quarter as large.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.3335<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta g_z(0)\\approx0.3335\\,\\mathrm{mGal}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Changes in source depth, shape, extent and contrast can compensate\neach other in the data: the inverse problem is non-unique.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The source is treated as a point mass below a planar survey line.\nReal bodies have extent; Earth curvature, topography and noise matter.\nGravity anomalies alone do not determine rock magnetisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Field anomalies constrain subsurface contrasts, but do not uniquely\nidentify hidden bodies.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Hydrostatic Atmosphere and\nTemperature Gradient<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-201-essentials-of-geophysics-fall-2004/resources/ch2/\">MIT OCW – Essentials of Geophysics – ch2.pdf (Ressourcenseite)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 118–132; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L2 – Gravity, Magnetic Fields and Geophysical Anomalies · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-201-essentials-of-geophysics-fall-2004/resources/ch3pdf/\">MIT OCW – Essentials of Geophysics – ch3pdf.pdf (Ressourcenseite)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 121; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L2 – Gravity, Magnetic Fields and Geophysical Anomalies · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-201-essentials-of-geophysics-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Essentials of Geophysics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: ch. 2, lines 136–146; chapter outline; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L2 – Gravity, Magnetic Fields and Geophysical Anomalies · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M21-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M21-L3 – Hydrostatic Atmosphere and Temperature Gradient<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Bachelor: Geophysics and Climate<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4: fluids; M5: ideal gases; M8:\ndifferential equations. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the barometric pressure\nprofile and distinguish an isothermal model from a real lapse rate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Air pressure generally decreases with altitude because less\natmospheric mass remains overhead. Temperature also changes, so air\ndensity is not constant. Hydrostatic balance describes the vertical\npressure distribution when vertical acceleration is small compared with\ngravity. It does not require the entire atmosphere to be motionless.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>A thin layer of air satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dp/dz=-\\rho g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCombining this with the ideal-gas equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\rho R_sT<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{dp}{dz}=-\\frac{pg}{R_sT}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nOnly if temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand gravitational acceleration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare constant does integration yield\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>g<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z)=p_0\\exp(-z/H),\\qquad H=\\frac{R_sT}{g}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nFor dry air\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>287<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\" mathvariant=\"normal\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s\\approx287\\,\\mathrm{J/(kg\\,K)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith a varying temperature profile the correct expression is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>z<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>g<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln[p(z)/p_0]=-\\int_0^z g/[R_sT(z^{\\prime})]\\,dz^{\\prime}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe tropospheric temperature decrease is not one universal constant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Take the illustrative assumptions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>288<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=288\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1013<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_0=1013\\,\\mathrm{hPa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe scale height is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>8426<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H\\approx8426\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=5\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1013<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5000<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8426<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>560<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx1013\\exp(-5000/8426)\\approx560\\,\\mathrm{hPa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne panel shows the exponential isothermal pressure curve; the other\nshows a separate idealised linear temperature profile, not the\ntemperature of that isothermal column.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_010_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"An isothermal pressure-versus-height curve beside a separate illustrative temperature lapse-rate plot.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">An isothermal pressure-versus-height\ncurve beside a separate illustrative temperature lapse-rate\nplot.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Isothermal pressure curve and separately plotted temperature\nprofile.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The curves are separate teaching models, not one self-consistently\ncoupled atmospheric column.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Derive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H=R_sT/g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom hydrostatic balance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(H)/p_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an isothermal atmosphere.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do an isothermal pressure model and a nonzero tropospheric lapse\nrate not describe one fully consistent atmospheric column?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Insert\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=p/(R_sT)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nseparate variables and integrate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dp/p=-dz/H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.368<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(H)/p_0=e^{-1}\\approx0.368<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=T(z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe pressure-equation integrand changes; a single constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nno longer solves it exactly.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>Vertical accelerations, water vapour and its effect on the effective\nspecific gas constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngravity changes and atmospheric circulation are omitted. The two curves\ndeliberately illustrate different approximations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Hydrostatic balance plus the equation of state explains the pressure\ndecrease; temperature structure controls its detailed profile.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Planetary Radiation Budget and\nEffective Temperature<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-003-atmosphere-ocean-and-climate-dynamics-fall-2008/pages/calendar/\">MIT OCW – Atmosphere, Ocean and Climate Dynamics – Calendar<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 142 ff., sessions 2–6; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L3 – Hydrostatic Atmosphere and Temperature Gradient · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-003-atmosphere-ocean-and-climate-dynamics-fall-2008/resources/homework3/\">MIT OCW – Problem set 3: Hydrostatic balance and lapse rate<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 143–157; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L3 – Hydrostatic Atmosphere and Temperature Gradient · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M21-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M21-L4 – Planetary Radiation Budget and Effective Temperature<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Bachelor: Geophysics and Climate<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5: thermodynamics; M7: radiation;\nM13: thermal spectra. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Set up a global radiation budget\nand compute the effective emission temperature.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Earth receives solar radiation, reflects part of it and emits thermal\nenergy to space. A balanced long-term global mean does not imply local\nequilibrium everywhere and at all times. Averaging over a spherical\nplanet distributes incoming power over four times its illuminated\ncross-sectional area. Atmospheric downward thermal radiation is an\nadditional process within the system, not an extra incoming solar\nsource.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the solar irradiance on a surface normal to sunlight,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe planetary albedo and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe Stefan–Boltzmann constant. A stationary global top-of-atmosphere\nmodel has\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><msubsup><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{abs}}=\\frac{S_0}{4}(1-\\alpha),\\qquad F_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\sigma T_e^4.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nEquating these fluxes gives\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>σ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_e=\\left[\\frac{S_0(1-\\alpha)}{4\\sigma}\\right]^{1/4}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nThe effective radiating temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not automatically the mean surface temperature. Greenhouse gases and\nclouds determine the altitudes and wavelengths from which radiation\nescapes to space.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>In a rounded teaching example, take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1360<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0=1360\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe average incoming solar flux is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">340\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nreflected flux\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>102<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">102\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand absorbed flux\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>238<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">238\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>254.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_e\\approx254.5\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram separates incoming, reflected and outgoing fluxes at the top\nof the atmosphere.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_219_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Global radiation budget showing 340 incoming, 102 reflected and 238 outgoing watts per square metre.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Global radiation budget showing 340\nincoming, 102 reflected and 238 outgoing watts per square\nmetre.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Rounded educational top-of-atmosphere energy budget; not live\nmeasurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{abs}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1400<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_0=1400\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif absorbed flux increases by a factor of 16?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot necessarily the average near-surface air temperature?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1400<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>262.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{abs}}=1400\\cdot0.75/4=262.5\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msubsup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_e\\propto F_{\\mathrm{abs}}^{1/4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe temperature doubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The level and spectrum of radiation emitted to space differ from\nsurface conditions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>This budget is globally averaged, stationary and spectrally\nsimplified. Its values are rounded teaching inputs, not live\nobservations. The outgoing thermal arrow represents radiation escaping\nat the top of the atmosphere, not necessarily photons travelling\ndirectly from the surface.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>A simple budget defines effective emission temperature; predicting\nsurface temperature requires atmosphere–ocean physics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Atmospheric Circulation and the\nCoriolis Effect<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/climate-and-earths-energy-budget/\">NASA Earth Observatory – Climate and Earth’s Energy Budget<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 328–357; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L4 – Planetary Radiation Budget and Effective Temperature · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-003-atmosphere-ocean-and-climate-dynamics-fall-2008/pages/calendar/\">MIT OCW – Atmosphere, Ocean and Climate Dynamics – Calendar<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 142 ff., sessions 2–6; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L4 – Planetary Radiation Budget and Effective Temperature · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M21-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M21-L5 – Atmospheric Circulation and the Coriolis Effect<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Bachelor: Geophysics and Climate<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M3: rotating frames; M4: fluid\nmotion; M21-L3. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain how planetary rotation\naffects large-scale airflow and interpret a simplified Hadley cell.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Unequal solar heating creates differences in temperature and pressure\nacross latitudes. In a schematic tropical circulation, warm moist air\nrises near the equator and sinks farther poleward in an idealised\nsubtropical region. Earth rotation deflects horizontally moving air\nrelative to the rotating surface. This does not explain every local\ncloud, but frames major circulation patterns.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>In Earth’s rotating frame, Coriolis acceleration is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒂<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">𝛀<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol a_C=-2\\boldsymbol\\Omega\\times\\boldsymbol v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor horizontal motion at latitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe local Coriolis parameter is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=2\\Omega\\sin\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAway from the equator, on sufficiently large spatial and temporal\nscales, an approximate geostrophic balance is\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mfrac><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\boldsymbol k\\times\\boldsymbol v_g=-\\frac{1}{\\rho}\\nabla_h p.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nThe Coriolis force does no kinetic work because it is perpendicular to\nvelocity. Hadley circulation reflects heating, convection and\nangular-momentum transport; a simple two-dimensional picture omits\nseasonal shifts and continents.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.292<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega=7.292\\cdot10^{-5}\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.03<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\approx1.03\\cdot10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA horizontal speed of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthen corresponds to a Coriolis acceleration of about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.03<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.03\\cdot10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows a qualitative overturning circulation and\ndistinguishes rising air, upper transport, descent and surface\nreturn.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_009_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A schematic tropical circulation cell between the equator and subtropics, with ascent, upper flow, descent and surface return.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A schematic tropical circulation cell\nbetween the equator and subtropics, with ascent, upper flow, descent and\nsurface return.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Idealised tropical Hadley cell, strongly simplified to two\ndimensions.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=0^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|a_C|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=20\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why may geostrophic balance fail for a small, short-lived\nvortex?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=2\\Omega\\sin0^\\circ=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|a_C|=fv=2\\cdot10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Acceleration, friction and convection may dominate over the Coriolis\nforce on small or fast scales.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The diagram is neither an annual average of the real ITCZ nor a\nforecast. Geostrophic balance neglects friction and acceleration; it\nfails near the equator as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\rightarrow0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Large-scale winds connect thermal forcing with planetary rotation;\ncirculation cells are useful but simplified models.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Remote Sensing with Active and\nPassive Sensors<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-003-atmosphere-ocean-and-climate-dynamics-fall-2008/pages/calendar/\">MIT OCW – Atmosphere, Ocean and Climate Dynamics – Calendar<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 142 ff., sessions 2–6; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L5 – Atmospheric Circulation and the Coriolis Effect · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M21-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M21-L6 – Remote Sensing with Active and Passive Sensors<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Bachelor: Geophysics and Climate<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6: electromagnetic waves; M7:\noptics; M17: inverse models. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Differentiate active and passive\nsensors and calculate a basic time-of-flight range.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Remote sensing infers properties of a target without physical\ncontact. Passive instruments detect available radiation, such as\nreflected sunlight or thermal emission. Active systems transmit a signal\nand receive its scattered return. Even a precise recorded signal does\nnot directly provide a unique material property: calibration, a forward\nmodel and often inversion are needed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal monostatic radar, the propagation delay\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbetween transmitted and received pulses gives\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\frac{v\\Delta t}{2},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis signal speed. The factor of one half accounts for the outward and\nreturn paths. In air or vacuum one often approximates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\approx c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut other media or substantial atmospheric delays require corrections. A\npassive observation can be written schematically as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℱ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\mathcal F(x)+\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith target properties\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nradiative forward operator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℱ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand noise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInverting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not necessarily give a unique\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>With a round-trip delay\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=100\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the educational approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=c=3.0\\cdot10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe radar range is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=15\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure separates passive incoming radiation from active transmission\nand echo. It does not depict a real satellite mission or a\nscale-accurate optical path.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_240_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Side-by-side passive radiation detection and active radar range measurement with outgoing and returning paths.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Side-by-side passive radiation detection\nand active radar range measurement with outgoing and returning\npaths.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Principle of active and passive remote sensing; geometry is\nschematic.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the range if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=40\\,\\mathrm{\\mu s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=3.0\\cdot10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is there a factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the radar range formula?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give one reason two physically different surfaces might have the\nsame measured radiance.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=3.0\\cdot10^8\\cdot40\\cdot10^{-6}/2=6000\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The recorded time includes propagation to the target and back.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Different combinations of temperature, emissivity, illumination or\natmospheric effects can give similar observations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The range formula assumes one identifiable return along a simple\npath; multipath, resolution, speckle, scattering cross section and\nrefraction are neglected. Passive radiance alone is not a unique\nmaterial fingerprint.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Active sensors create their own signals; passive sensors receive\nexisting radiation. Both require physical interpretation and uncertainty\nanalysis.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Climate Archives, Proxy Data and\nUncertainty<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/remotesensing.html\">NOAA National Ocean Service – What is remote sensing?<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 5–15; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L6 – Remote Sensing with Active and Passive Sensors · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/general/what-are-passive-and-active-sensors/\">NASA – What are passive and active sensors?<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 254–263 (Passive Sensors); Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L6 – Remote Sensing with Active and Passive Sensors · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-201-essentials-of-geophysics-fall-2004/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Essentials of Geophysics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: ch. 2, lines 136–146; chapter outline; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L6 – Remote Sensing with Active and Passive Sensors · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M21-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M21-L7 – Climate Archives, Proxy Data and Uncertainty<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Bachelor: Geophysics and Climate<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M16: uncertainty analysis; M17: data\nmodels; M21-L4. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Critically interpret indirect\nclimate reconstructions and correctly weight independent estimates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Before widespread instrumental climate observations, environmental\ninformation is recovered from archives such as ice cores, tree rings,\ncorals and sediments. Measured isotope ratios, ring widths and chemical\nmarkers are proxies, not direct measurements of the desired temperature.\nReconstruction needs chronology, calibration, a defensible physical or\nstatistical response model and systematic-error assessment. Individual\narchives cover different locations and time spans.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>A simple linear calibration model writes proxy observation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=a+bT+\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith target temperature quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand error term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand nonzero\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare known,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>T<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat T=(q-a)/b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwith fixed calibration coefficients the proxy-only uncertainty is\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_T=u_q/|b|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalibration and dating uncertainties must be added. For two or more\n<strong>independent<\/strong>, unbiased temperature estimates with\nstandard uncertainties\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ninverse-variance weighting gives\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>T<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mover><mi>T<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msubsup><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar T=\\frac{\\sum_i T_i/u_i^2}{\\sum_i1/u_i^2},\\qquad u_{\\bar T}=\\left(\\sum_i1/u_i^2\\right)^{-1/2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nCorrelated errors invalidate this simple uncertainty formula.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose two fictitious, assumed independent temperature anomalies are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_1=1.2\\pm0.3\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_2=0.8\\pm0.4\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWeights are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mn>0.3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/0.3^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mn>0.4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/0.4^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>T<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.056<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar T=1.056\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mover><mi>T<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.24<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{\\bar T}=0.24\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe plot is an entirely synthetic proxy reconstruction with an\nillustrative uncertainty band. It is neither ice-core data nor a\nvalidated historic temperature estimate.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_041_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic proxy reconstruction against age with sample markers and an illustrative uncertainty band.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Synthetic proxy reconstruction against\nage with sample markers and an illustrative uncertainty\nband.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Entirely synthetic teaching reconstruction.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Not historical climate data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>T<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin appropriately compatible units.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does inverse-variance weighting give for two independent\nestimates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\pm1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pm1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why could common dating errors make the formally combined\nuncertainty overoptimistic?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>T<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat T=(5-1)/2=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the temperature unit defined by the calibration.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>T<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar T=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mover><mi>T<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.707<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{\\bar T}=1/\\sqrt2\\approx0.707<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Shared errors are correlated; averaging does not eliminate them as\nif they were independent.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The synthetic markers do not describe actual climate history.\nUnbiasedness, normal errors, temporal stationarity, proxy independence\nand calibration validity require scrutiny. Chronology uncertainty acts\nalong the horizontal time axis and is not captured by a vertical\ntemperature-error band alone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Climate archives provide indirect evidence of past environments.\nQuantitative reconstructions must expose calibration and the full\nuncertainty structure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/paleoclimatology/climate-reconstruction\">NOAA NCEI – Climate Reconstruction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 2–14, 40–59; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L7 – Climate Archives, Proxy Data and Uncertainty · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/climate-and-earths-energy-budget/\">NASA Earth Observatory – Climate and Earth’s Energy Budget<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Evidence location: lines 328–357; Subject relevance to lesson: M21-L7 – Climate Archives, Proxy Data and Uncertainty · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
